.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH MOUNT 1M
.SH NAME
mount, umount \- mount and dismount file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/mount
[ special directory [
.B \-r
] ]
.PP
.B /etc/umount
special
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mount
announces to the system that a removable file system
is present on the device
.IR special ,
which must be a
.B block
special file \-
.I /dev/md1
and not the raw
.IR /dev/rmd1 .
The file
.I directory
must exist already; it
must be a directory (unless the root of the
mounted file system is not a directory).
It becomes the name of the newly mounted root,
and whatever was underneath it is unreachable until the file system is dismounted.
The optional
.B \-r
mounts the file system read-only.
.PP
.I Umount
announces to the system that the removable file system previously
mounted on device
.I special
is to be removed.
It calls
.IR sync (2)
first.
.PP
These commands
maintain a table of mounted devices,
.IR /etc/mtab .
If invoked without an argument,
.I mount
prints it.
.PP
Physically write-protected file
systems must be mounted read-only
or errors will occur when access times are updated,
whether or not any explicit write is attempted.
There are no magnetic tape file systems here \- this system has no tape.
.SH "HOW MANY AT ONCE"
.B Seven.
.B NMOUNT
in
.RI < sys/param.h >
is the number of file systems the kernel can have mounted,
it is 8 on this system, and the root holds one of the eight.
An eighth
.I mount
gets
.B EBUSY
\- which is also what a device that is already mounted,
and a mount point that is already somebody's working directory,
both answer.
.PP
Each mounted file system holds one buffer of the cache for its superblock
for as long as it is mounted,
so the seven are not free:
.B NBUF
is 16 and every mount takes one of them out of circulation
until the file system is unmounted.
.SH "WHAT IS REFUSED"
.TP
.B ENOTBLK
.I Special
is not a block special file.
This is the mistake the command invites on this system,
every other file system program \-
.IR mkfs (1M),
.IR fsck (1M),
.IR icheck (1M)
\- taking the
.B raw
name instead.
.TP
.B EBUSY
See
.B "HOW MANY AT ONCE"
above.
For
.IR umount ,
somebody is still on the file system:
an open file or a process whose working directory is inside it.
The shell that typed the command counts, so
.B "cd /mnt; /etc/umount /dev/md1"
never works.
.TP
.B EINVAL
For
.IR mount ,
there is no file system on
.IR special .
For
.IR umount ,
nothing is mounted on it.
.TP
.B EIO
The superblock could not be read at all.
.SH FILES
/etc/mtab: mount table
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mount(2),
mtab(5),
mkfs(1M),
fsck(1M)
.SH BUGS
Mounting a root directory on a non-directory
makes some apparently good pathnames invalid.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.B "mounting a file system full of garbage no longer crashes the system,"
which is what this page said for as long as there was a v7.
The kernel checks the superblock at mount time \-
.IR sbcheck (),
.I kernel/alloc.c
\- for the magic number, the geometry this system was built for, an i-list
extent inside the volume and free counts within their caches,
and refuses with
.B EINVAL
after printing on the console what it disliked.
v7 had no superblock magic number at all;
this file system has one, and
.IR filsys (5)
records it.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.B "\-r is parsed."
v7 mounts read-only if it is given any third argument whatever and never looks
at what it is, so
.B "mount /dev/md1 /mnt rw"
mounts read-only there.
Here anything but
.B \-r
is refused.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.B "a umount whose special file is not in the table still succeeds."
v7 prints
.B "not in mount table"
and exits 1, having already unmounted the file system.
This one says so on the standard error and exits 0:
.IR mtab (5)
has said since v7 that the table is there so people can look at it,
and an exit status claiming the unmount failed when it succeeded is worse
than a stale table.
